WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.800
So whereas in transfer learning, you have a sequential process where you learn from task A and then transfer that to task B,

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:13.320
in multi-task learning, you start off simultaneously trying to have one neural network do several things at the same time,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.160
and then each of these tasks helps hopefully all of the other tasks.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.720
Let's look at an example.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.840
Let's say you're building an autonomous vehicle, building a self-driving car,

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.280
then your self-driving car will need to detect several different things,

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:42.340
such as pedestrians, detect other cars, detect stop signs, and also detect traffic lights, and also other things.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:49.420
So for example, in this example on the left, there is a stop sign in this image and there is a car in this image,

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:53.020
but there aren't any pedestrians or traffic lights.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:01:02.620
So if this image is an input, for example, xi, then instead of having one label yi, you would actually have four labels.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:08.700
In this example, there are no pedestrians, there is a car, there is a stop sign, and there are no traffic lights.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:12.540
And if you're trying to detect other things, then maybe yi has even more dimensions.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.340
But for now, let's take a look at these four.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.940
So yi is a four by one vector.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:22.980
And if you look at the training sets labels as a whole, then, you know,

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:30.500
similar to before, we'll stack the training data's labels horizontally as follows.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:34.860
y1 up to ym, except that now yi is a four by one vector.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:39.300
So each of these is a tall column vector.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:44.220
And so y, this matrix capital Y, is now a four by m matrix.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:49.620
Whereas previously, when y was a single row number, this would have been a one by m matrix.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:54.980
So what you can do is now train a neural network to predict these values of y.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:02:00.780
So you can have a neural network input x and output now a four dimensional value for y.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:04.340
Notice here for the output layer, I've drawn four nodes.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:10.540
And so the first node, when we try to predict, is there a pedestrian in this picture?

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:13.340
The second output will predict is there a car here?

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.580
Predict is there a stop sign?

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:20.700
And this will predict maybe is there a traffic light?

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:25.940
So y hat here is four dimensional.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:31.860
So to train this neural network, you now need to define the loss for the neural network.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:39.060
And so given a predicted output y hat i, which is four by one dimensional,

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:46.380
the loss average over your entire training set would be one over m,

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:59.020
sum from i equals one through m, sum from j equals one through four of the losses of the individual predictions.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:04.220
So it's just summing over the four components of pedestrian, car, stop sign, traffic light.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:14.440
And this script L is the usual logistic loss.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:31.660
So just to write this out, this is negative y j i log y hat j i minus one minus y log one minus y hat.

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:40.380
And the main difference compared to the earlier binary classification examples is that you're now summing over j equals one through four.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:46.020
And the main difference between this and softmax regression is that unlike softmax regression,

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:55.260
which assigned a single label to single example, this one image can have multiple labels.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:04.620
So you're not saying that each image is either a picture of a pedestrian or picture of a car, picture of a stop sign, picture of a traffic light.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:09.420
You're asking for each picture, does it have a pedestrian or a car stop sign or traffic light?

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.780
And multiple objects could appear in the same image.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:19.220
In fact, in the example on the previous slide, we had both a car and a stop sign in that image, but no pedestrians and traffic lights.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:22.460
So you're not assigning a single label to an image.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:31.220
You're going through the different classes and asking, you know, for each of the classes, does that class, does that type of object appear in the image?

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:37.940
So that's why I'm saying that with this setting, one image can have multiple labels.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:45.780
If you train a neural network to minimize this cost function, you are carrying out multitask learning.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:53.700
Because what you're doing is building a single neural network that is looking at each image and basically solving four problems.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:05:00.020
It's trying to tell you, does each image have each of these four objects in it?

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:06.500
And one other thing you could have done is just train four separate neural networks instead of train one network to do four things.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:12.420
But if some of the earlier features in your network can be shared between these different types of objects,

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:22.820
then you find that training one neural network to do four things results in better performance than training four completely separate neural networks to do the four tasks separately.

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:26.500
So that's the power of multitask learning.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:33.220
And one other detail, you know, so far I've described this algorithm as if every image had every single label.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:39.220
It turns out that multitask learning also works even if some of the images were labeled only with some of the objects.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:45.220
So the first training example, let's say someone, your laborer had told you there's a pedestrian, there's no car,

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:48.900
but they didn't bother to label whether or not there's a stop sign or whether or not there's a traffic light.

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:55.220
And maybe for the second example, there is a pedestrian, there is a car, but again the laborer, when they looked at that image,

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:59.620
they just didn't label it with whether it had a stop sign or whether the traffic light and so on.

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:05.860
And maybe some examples are fully labeled and maybe some examples, they were just labeling for the presence and absence of cars.

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:08.660
So there are some question marks and so on.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:14.980
So with a dataset like this, you can still train your learning algorithm to do four tasks at the same time,

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:21.060
even when some images have only a subset of the labels and others are sort of question marks or don't cares.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:26.100
And the way you train your algorithm, even when some of these labels are question marks are really unlabeled,

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:41.160
is that in this sum over j from 1 to 4, you would sum only over values of j with a 0 or 1 label.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:46.520
So whenever there's a question mark, you just omit that term from summation,

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:50.440
but just sum over only the values where there is a label.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.800
And so that allows you to use datasets like this as well.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:57.240
So when does multitask learning make sense?

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.320
So when does multitask learning make sense?

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:03.320
I say it makes sense usually when three things hold true.

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:08.280
One is if you're training on a set of tasks that could benefit from having shared low-level features.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:15.080
So for the autonomous driving example, it makes sense that recognizing traffic lights and cars and pedestrians,

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:21.400
those should have similar features that could also help you recognize stop signs because these are all features of roads.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:25.800
Second, this is less of a hard and fast rule, so this isn't always true,

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:33.640
but what I see for a lot of successful multitask learning settings is that the amount of data you have for each task is quite similar.

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:40.200
So if you recall from transfer learning, you learn from some task A and transfer it to some task B.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:47.720
So if you have a million examples for task A, then and 1000 examples for task B,

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:55.240
then all the knowledge you learn from that million examples could really help augment the much smaller data set you have for task B.

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:58.360
Well, how about multitask learning?

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:03.960
In multitask learning, you usually have a lot more tasks than just two, so maybe previously we had four tasks,

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:10.120
but let's say you have a hundred tasks and you're going to do multitask learning to try to recognize

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:12.520
a hundred different types of objects at the same time.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:17.240
So what you might find is that you might have a thousand examples per task,

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:23.400
and so if you focus on the performance of just one task, let's focus on the performance on the hundredth task,

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:25.640
which you can call A100.

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:28.440
If you had trained to do this final task in isolation,

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:34.200
you would have had just a thousand examples to train on this one task, this one of a hundred tasks,

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:42.200
but by training on these 99 other tasks, these in aggregate have 99,000 training examples.

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:47.560
Which could be a big boost, could give a lot of knowledge to augment this otherwise relatively small

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:52.360
1000 example training set that you have for task A100.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:57.480
And symmetrically, every one of the other 99 tasks can provide some data or provide some knowledge

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:01.080
to help every one of the other tasks in this list of 100 tasks.

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:09.560
So the second bullet isn't a hard and fast rule, but what I tend to look at is if you focus on any one task,

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:15.400
for that to get a big boost for multi-task learning, the other tasks in aggregate need to have

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:18.040
quite a lot more data than for that one task.

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:23.240
And so one way to satisfy that is if you've logged a task, like we have in this example on the right,

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:25.960
and if the amount of data you have on each task is quite similar.

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:31.560
But the key really is that if you already have a thousand examples for one task,

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:35.800
then for all of the other tasks, you better have a lot more than 1000 examples

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:39.880
if those other tasks are meant to help you do better on this final task.

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:45.160
And finally, multi-task learning tends to make more sense when you can train a big enough neural

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:47.400
network to do well on all the tasks.

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:51.720
So the alternative to multi-task learning would be to train a separate neural network

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:55.880
for each task. So rather than training one neural net for pedestrian, car, stop sign,

00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:59.080
and traffic light detection, you could have trained one neural network for pedestrian

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:03.480
detection, one neural network for car detection, one neural network for stop sign detection,

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:05.480
and one neural network for traffic light detection.

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:11.800
So what a researcher, Rich Karaner, found many years ago was that the only times that multi-task

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:16.680
learning hurts performance compared to training separate neural networks is if your neural

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:21.400
network isn't big enough. But if you can train a big enough neural network, then multi-task

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:27.080
learning certainly should not or should very rarely hurt performance, but hopefully it will

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:31.560
actually help performance compared to if you are training neural networks to do these different

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:32.600
tasks in isolation.

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:37.960
So that's it for multi-task learning. In practice, multi-task learning is used much

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:42.520
less often than transfer learning. I see a lot of applications of transfer learning

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:46.600
where you have a problem you want to solve with a small amount of data. So you find a

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:50.840
related problem where you have a lot of data to learn something and transfer that to this new

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:55.880
problem. But multi-task learning is just more rare that you have a huge set of tasks you

00:10:55.880 --> 00:11:00.120
want to use, that you want to do well on. You can train all of those tasks at the same time.

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:05.320
Maybe the one example is computer vision. In object detection, I see more applications of

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:09.240
multi-task learning where one neural network trained to detect a whole bunch of objects at

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:12.680
the same time works better than different neural networks trained separately to detect

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:17.000
different objects. But I would say that on average, transfer learning is used much

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:21.880
more today than multi-task learning. But both are useful tools to have in your arsenal.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:27.400
So to summarize, multi-task learning enables you to train one neural network to do many

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:31.800
tasks and this can give you better performance than you would to do the tasks in isolation.

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:38.360
Now one note of caution. In practice, I see that transfer learning is used much more often than

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:42.280
multi-task learning. So I do see a lot of tasks where if you want to solve a machine

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:46.920
learning problem but you have a relatively small data set, then transfer learning can really help

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:50.920
where if you find a related problem but you have a much bigger data set, you can train a neural

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:54.680
network from there and then transfer it to the problem where you have very low data.

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:59.560
So transfer learning is used a lot today. There are some applications of transfer of

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:04.280
multi-task learning as well but multi-task learning I think is used much less often than

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:09.720
transfer learning. And maybe the one exception is computer vision object detection where I do see

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:13.800
a lot of applications of training a neural network to detect lots of different objects

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:17.960
and that works better than training separate neural networks to detect individual objects.

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:22.680
But on average, I think that even though transfer learning and multi-task learning often

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:28.360
are presented in a similar way, in practice I've seen a lot more applications of transfer learning

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:33.320
than a multi-task learning. I think because often it's just difficult to set up or to find

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:37.320
so many different tasks that you would actually want to train a single neural network for. Again,

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:42.200
with some sort of computer vision object detection examples being the most notable exception.

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:47.560
So that's it for multi-task learning. Multi-task learning and transfer learning are both

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:53.080
important tools to have in your tool bank. And finally, I'd like to move on to discuss

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:57.880
end-to-end deep learning. So let's go on to the next video to discuss end-to-end learning.
